I"=<p>Let our model be $y = X\beta + \epsilon$. Here, the design matrix $X$ is an augmented one. Our objective is to estimate the regression coefficient $\beta$ with $\hat{\beta}$.</p>

<p>By the least-square or the maximum likelihood, $\hat{\beta} = (X’X)^{-1}X’y$ is the estimate. what happens if we centered the data before fitting the model? will the values of coefficients change? (Spoiler alert: the title)</p>

<h3 id="1-centering-the-predictors">1. centering the predictors</h3>
<p>Since the $\mathbb{1}$ column vector inside the design matrix is not affected by centering, we will split $X$ into $\mathbb{1}$ and the matrix of observed predictor values, then center the values in the latter.</p>

<p>[X_c = \begin{bmatrix} 
        \mathbb{1} &amp; X_1
        \end{bmatrix}]</p>

<p>is the centered and augmented design matrix.</p>

<p>Our regression coefficients for the centered form is specified as</p>

<p>[\beta_c = \begin{bmatrix} 
            \alpha <br />
            \beta_1 <br />
            \vdots <br />
            \beta_p
          \end{bmatrix}
        = \begin{bmatrix}
            \alpha <br />
            \boldsymbol{\beta_1}
          \end{bmatrix}]</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>[\alpha = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \bar{x_1} + \cdots + \beta_p \bar{x_p}, ::
\boldsymbol{\beta_1} = \begin{bmatrix}
                     \beta_1 <br />
                     \vdots <br />
                     \beta_p
                   \end{bmatrix}.]</p>

<p>Then our model becomes</p>

<p>[\begin{align}
y &amp;= X_c \beta_c + \epsilon <br />
  &amp;= \alpha \cdot \mathbb{1} + X_1 \boldsymbol{\beta}_1 + \epsilon .
\end{align}]</p>

<p>Using this to fit the model yields the same coefficient values <strong>except</strong> the intercept ($\hat{\beta_0}$ from the original model, $\hat{\alpha}$ from the centered one).</p>

